former President Jimmy Carter says  senator McCain's death does not signal  the end of bipartisanship in Washington  we spoke with President Carter yesterday  at a Habitat for Humanity worksite in  Indiana John McCain also served in the  Navy of course passed away recently what  are your thoughts on his passing  well John McCain's service in the Navy  made me proud his personal heroism has  been proven and I think here's a basic  integrity his commitment to the truth no  matter if it was politically unpopular I  was accepted by him as something that  had to be done so I think that John  McCain was admirable as a naval officer  as a human being and as a member of the  US Senate when you look at the Senate as  it has changed when you were in office  some people are saying that John  McCain's death represents the end of  kind of an era  I wouldn't say that maybe temporarily  but I have confidence in the future of  the United States of America you know  we've placed problems even more than we  have now in the past we've overcome them  mr. Carter's survived his own battle  with brain tumors in 2015 we'll have  more of our interview with President  Carter in our next hour here his vision  for America's future and what motivates  him to continue volunteering with  Habitat for Humanity after nearly 35  years
 

